 "transcript": {
  "answer": "No, not yet. Inflation is above the RBA's 2–3% target and has been rising for about a year.\n\n**Latest figures (ABS monthly CPI, August 2026):**\n- **Headline CPI: 4.0% year-on-year**, up from 3.5% in July. It peaked at 4.6% in March 2026.\n- **Trimmed mean: 3.6%**, the RBA's preferred measure of underlying inflation. This has gone up steadily from 2.8% in June 2025 to 3.3% around the turn of the year, then to 3.6% from June to August 2026.\n\n**How we got here:**\n- In mid-2025 it looked like inflation was under control. Headline CPI fell to 1.9% in June 2025, and quarterly trimmed mean was 2.7% in the June quarter.\n- Since then both measures have risen. Underlying inflation has been above 3% every month since July 2025, so the rise isn't just a few volatile items.\n\n**What the RBA has done:**\n- It cut the cash rate from 4.35% to 3.60% over 2025.\n- It then reversed course, raising rates between February and May 2026 back to **4.35%**, where the rate has stayed through September 2026.\n\n**Bottom line:** Headline inflation is a full point above the top of the target band. The more telling underlying measure is 0.6 points above it and still rising. It's a good sign that the trimmed mean has held at 3.6% for three months, which may mean the rate rises are starting to work. But inflation won't be under control until underlying inflation turns down towards the 2.5% midpoint of the target. The September CPI (out in late October) will be the next test.",
